1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,600
This is Doing and Feeling, the long version. Episode 4, Life as Computation. In 1948, at a symposium in Pasadena, the mathematician John von Neumann asked a question that sounds like an engineering puzzle and turns out to be a question about life.

2
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:20,060
Could a machine build a copy of itself?

3
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,260
The difficulty is a regress.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:25,760
To build a copy, the machine needs a description of itself.

5
00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:28,220
But the description is part of the machine,

6
00:00:28,220 --> 00:00:32,340
so it must contain a description of the description, and so on forever.

7
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:36,740
Von Neumann's way out was to use one description in two different ways.

8
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:40,820
A constructor reads the description and builds what it specifies.

9
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:45,080
A copier duplicates the description blindly, without interpreting it.

10
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:47,560
Build the offspring from the instructions,

11
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:50,160
copy the instructions without reading them,

12
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:53,100
hand the copy over, and the regress disappears.

13
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:56,120
And if the copying is occasionally imperfect,

14
00:00:56,560 --> 00:01:00,340
the offspring differ, which supplies the variation evolution needs.

15
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:04,520
This was five years before Watson and Crick described the double helix,

16
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:07,440
yet it closely parallels how cells use DNA.

17
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:11,920
Genes are read, transcribed, and translated to build proteins,

18
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:17,780
and the same DNA is copied, base by base, without being interpreted, to make a new cell.

19
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:22,640
von Neumann had worked out the logical architecture of life from first principles.

20
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:28,380
This episode makes the strongest case I can for one claim and then tests it.

21
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:33,180
The claim is that life, intelligence, and computation are a single continuum.

22
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:34,840
It has four steps.

23
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:40,260
First, replicators arise naturally wherever computation and interaction meet.

24
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:47,320
leaps in complexity come from merging. Third, anything that keeps itself going must regulate,

25
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:53,220
and good regulation requires a model of what is being regulated, which makes prediction the core

26
00:01:53,220 --> 00:01:59,600
of being alive. Fourth, the hardest things to predict are other predictors, so minds grew by

27
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:05,800
modeling other minds. The most ambitious recent statement of this view is a 2025 book by Blaise

28
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:12,240
Aguera e. Arcus, published by MIT Press and the Antikythera Project and freely readable online.

29
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:19,160
Its title is a question, what is intelligence? I'll use his argument as a spine. The test comes

30
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:24,240
at the end, and it is a serious one. If everything computes, does the claim say anything at all?

31
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:31,300
Start with replicators. Von Neumann went on to design a cell-for-producing pattern in a cellular

32
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:38,680
automaton, a grid of cells, each in one of 29 states, updated by local rules. It was published

33
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:46,180
after his death in 1966. In 1970, John Conway's Game of Life showed that two states and a handful

34
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:53,360
of rules could support gliders, oscillators, and, eventually, universal computation. In 1984,

35
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:58,960
Christopher Langton found a far simpler self-replicating pattern, now called Langton's

36
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:06,700
loops. Then came artificial life in software. In 1991, the ecologist Thomas Ray released Tiara,

37
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:11,740
a virtual computer filled with self-copying programs competing for processor time.

38
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:19,160
Parasites evolved that borrowed other programs' copying code and hosts evolved defenses. In 2003,

39
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:25,920
Richard Lensky, Charles Ofria, and colleagues used a platform called AVIDA to show how a complex

40
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:31,160
logical function could evolve through a series of simpler steps, some of them harmful when they

41
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:37,680
first arose. So the idea is old. But in Tiara, the first replicator was written by hand,

42
00:03:37,860 --> 00:03:44,020
and Avidor rewards programs for doing useful logic. That's what makes a 2024 experiment by

43
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:49,600
Aguera E. Arcus and colleagues at Google, including Ben Lory and Alexander Mordvincev.

44
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:55,440
So striking. They built a primordial soup of about 130,000 random programs,

45
00:03:55,920 --> 00:04:00,960
each 64 bytes long, written in a variant of a famously minimal programming language

46
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:07,100
whose name is unprintable. They called the variant BFF. Code and data lived on the same tape,

47
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:12,740
so programs could read and overwrite themselves and each other. The procedure was simple. Pick

48
00:04:12,740 --> 00:04:17,740
two programs at random, glue them together, run the result, split it back into two halves,

49
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:22,680
and return them to the soup. Repeat millions of times. There was no fitness function,

50
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:28,780
no reward, and no handwritten ancestor. For a long time, nothing much happened. Then,

51
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:34,660
in about 40% of runs, there was a sudden transition. Self-replicating programs appeared,

52
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:39,200
spread, and took over the soup, and the measured complexity of the population jumped.

53
00:04:39,660 --> 00:04:44,600
It happened even with random mutations switched off, so the replicators were assembled through

54
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:49,540
interaction and self-modification rather than found by luck in the initial noise.

55
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:53,460
Surely the instruction set is doing a lot of the work.

56
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:56,100
The authors tested exactly that.

57
00:04:56,620 --> 00:05:00,260
Replicators also emerged in versions built on the fourth language

58
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:04,120
and on the instruction set of the old Z80 microprocessor.

59
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:08,560
In a minimal language called Sublec, they never arose on their own,

60
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:11,300
though replicators planted by hand could spread.

61
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:13,140
So the substrate matters.

62
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:16,940
What the experiment shows is that under fairly generic conditions,

63
00:05:17,540 --> 00:05:23,860
replication can arise without design, and once it arises, dominates. What it does not show is how

64
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:31,260
chemistry did it, or that the soup goes on evolving open-ended novelty. Aguera E. Arcus's reading is

65
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:38,180
that life is an attractor that computation falls into. The second step is merging. In the classic

66
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:45,460
picture, complexity grows by small mutations filtered by competition. Aguera E. Arcus argues

67
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:54,520
symbiogenesis, the fusion of once-independent organisms. Around 1905 to 1910, the Russian

68
00:05:54,520 --> 00:06:00,800
botanist Konstantin Marischkowski proposed that chloroplasts, the photosynthetic machinery in

69
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:07,280
plant cells, were once free-living bacteria. The idea was largely ignored until Lin Margiulis

70
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:14,480
revived and extended it in a 1967 paper, published under the name Lin Sagan and reportedly rejected

71
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:21,340
by about 15 journals first. Today, it's textbook biology. The mitochondria in your cells descend

72
00:06:21,340 --> 00:06:26,620
from bacteria engulfed by an ancestral cell roughly 2 billion years ago, and they still

73
00:06:26,620 --> 00:06:34,020
carry small genomes of their own. In 1995, John Maynard Smith and Irsh Satmari generalized the

74
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:39,620
pattern in the major transitions in evolution, replicating molecules joining into chromosomes,

75
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:45,360
simple cells merging into complex ones, single cells forming multicellular bodies,

76
00:06:45,940 --> 00:06:50,880
solitary animals forming colonies, and human societies held together by language.

77
00:06:51,060 --> 00:06:54,920
In each transition, entities that once reproduced independently

78
00:06:54,920 --> 00:06:58,020
come to reproduce only as parts of a larger whole.

79
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:02,280
Aguera E. Arcus reads the BFF soup the same way,

80
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:05,420
with replicators assembled from cooperating fragments,

81
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:10,320
and he argues that humans and machine intelligence are entering such a transition now.

82
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:15,100
That last claim is a prediction, and we'll meet a rival reading of it shortly.

83
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:21,200
The third step is the one that turns life into prediction, and it runs through physics.

84
00:07:21,460 --> 00:07:27,120
In 1943, Erwin Schrödinger lectured in Dublin on a question he published as What is Life?

85
00:07:27,420 --> 00:07:31,220
How do living things stay ordered against the second law of thermodynamics?

86
00:07:31,220 --> 00:07:38,420
They feed, he said, on negative entropy, maintaining internal order by exporting disorder to their surroundings.

87
00:07:39,010 --> 00:07:47,920
He also guessed that heredity must be stored in an aperiodic crystal, orderly but not repetitive, an idea that inspired both Crick and Watson.

88
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:50,680
Here's the bridge I find most compelling.

89
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:59,440
In 2012, Suzanne Still and colleagues proved a theorem about a broad class of systems driven by a fluctuating environment.

90
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:03,560
inevitably retains information about the past.

91
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:06,640
Some of that information helps predict the future.

92
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:10,540
The rest, which they called nostalgia, is useless for prediction,

93
00:08:10,980 --> 00:08:15,820
and the energy the system wastes as heat is proportional to how much of it the system keeps.

94
00:08:16,340 --> 00:08:20,960
In other words, a memory that does not predict is thermodynamically wasteful.

95
00:08:21,420 --> 00:08:26,420
Efficiency and prediction are the same pressure, described in two vocabularies.

96
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:28,920
Which ties back to episode 3.

97
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:34,920
If good prediction is good compression, then life is under physical pressure to compress its world.

98
00:08:35,160 --> 00:08:40,040
And that's exactly where one recent attempt to define life has run into trouble.

99
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:47,020
In a 2023 paper in Nature, Lee Cronin, Sarah Walker, and colleagues argued for assembly theory,

100
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:51,480
which scores an object by the minimum number of joining steps needed to build it,

101
00:08:51,620 --> 00:08:56,760
and treats complex objects found in many copies as a signature of selection and life.

102
00:08:56,760 --> 00:09:02,220
Critics led by Hector Zaniel replied that the measure is essentially a weak form of compression,

103
00:09:02,700 --> 00:09:08,340
a cousin of the algorithms behind zip files, and that it neither explains nor quantifies selection.

104
00:09:08,860 --> 00:09:14,480
The assembly theorists reject that comparison, and the argument continues. Either way,

105
00:09:14,680 --> 00:09:19,980
it shows how quickly attempts to define life end up in the information theory of episode 3.

106
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:26,560
One more definition matters for later. In the early 1970s, the Chilean biologists

107
00:09:26,760 --> 00:09:33,420
Humberto Maturana and Francisco Varela, defined a living system as autopoietic, self-producing,

108
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:38,320
a network of processes that continually makes the very components that make it up.

109
00:09:38,540 --> 00:09:44,000
Notice the difference from von Neumann. His replicator is defined by a description at copies.

110
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:50,200
Autopoiesis is defined by a body that keeps rebuilding itself. That difference will become

111
00:09:50,200 --> 00:09:58,860
the center of the skeptics case in episode 13. Now regulation. In 1943, Archero Rosenbluth,

112
00:09:59,060 --> 00:10:04,240
Norbert Wiener, and Julian Bigelow published a short paper called Behavior, Purpose, and

113
00:10:04,240 --> 00:10:10,420
Teleology. Purposeful behavior, they proposed, is simply behavior controlled by negative feedback.

114
00:10:10,700 --> 00:10:15,640
A thermostat that turns on the heat when the room gets cold is, in this minimal sense,

115
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:24,120
pursuing a goal. Wiener named the field cybernetics in 1948. W. Ross Ashby added the law of requisite

116
00:10:24,120 --> 00:10:30,080
variety. A regulator must be able to produce at least as much variety as the disturbances it is

117
00:10:30,080 --> 00:10:36,200
trying to cancel. In 1970, Roger Canant and Ashby published a theorem with a wonderful title,

118
00:10:36,520 --> 00:10:42,160
Every Good Regulator of a System Must Be a Model of That System. The careful statement is narrower

119
00:10:42,160 --> 00:10:48,060
than the slogan. They showed that the simplest regulator achieving optimal results must map the

120
00:10:48,060 --> 00:10:54,260
system's states onto its own, in effect mirroring it. The slogan, that every controller secretly

121
00:10:54,260 --> 00:11:00,140
contains a world model, goes further than the proof. That's one justification for model-based

122
00:11:00,140 --> 00:11:06,620
reinforcement learning. If you want to control an environment well, learn a model of how it responds.

123
00:11:06,620 --> 00:11:13,380
And in 2025, Jonathan Richens and colleagues gave the idea a modern, precise form.

124
00:11:13,640 --> 00:11:18,840
They proved that any agent able to generalize across a wide enough range of multi-step,

125
00:11:19,100 --> 00:11:23,220
goal-directed tasks must have learned a predictive model of its environment,

126
00:11:23,850 --> 00:11:26,560
one that can in principle be extracted from its behavior,

127
00:11:26,860 --> 00:11:30,300
and that more capable agents require more accurate models.

128
00:11:30,540 --> 00:11:33,960
That's the bridge Aguera e. Arcus' argument needs.

129
00:11:34,660 --> 00:11:38,980
Survival requires regulation, flexible regulation requires a model,

130
00:11:39,220 --> 00:11:42,340
and a model that runs ahead of the present is a predictor.

131
00:11:43,180 --> 00:11:46,400
The fourth step, intelligence becomes social.

132
00:11:46,860 --> 00:11:50,160
Much of any animal's environment consists of other animals,

133
00:11:50,160 --> 00:11:53,820
and the hardest things in the world to predict are other predictors.

134
00:11:54,060 --> 00:12:00,040
In 1976, the psychologist Nicholas Humphrey published The Social Function of Intellect.

135
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:02,760
His argument was that for many primates,

136
00:12:02,980 --> 00:12:06,080
the practical problems of daily life are not that demanding,

137
00:12:06,320 --> 00:12:07,760
but social life is.

138
00:12:08,380 --> 00:12:10,780
Alliances, deception, reciprocity,

139
00:12:11,140 --> 00:12:12,560
remembering who did what to whom.

140
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:15,000
Primate intelligence, he proposed,

141
00:12:15,460 --> 00:12:17,680
evolved mainly to handle other primates.

142
00:12:18,180 --> 00:12:20,800
Richard Byrne and Andrew Whiten developed this

143
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:23,260
into the Machiavellian intelligence hypothesis,

144
00:12:23,760 --> 00:12:26,800
and Robin Dunbar found that across primate species,

145
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:29,380
the relative size of the neocortex

146
00:12:30,040 --> 00:12:36,320
typical social group size. Extrapolated to humans, it suggests a natural group of about 150,

147
00:12:36,880 --> 00:12:43,680
now called Dunbar's number. The correlation has been challenged too. A 2017 study of more than

148
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:50,880
140 primate species found that diet predicted brain size better than group size did. Aguera

149
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:56,800
e. archis turns this into an engine. If I predict you and you predict me, each of us gains by

150
00:12:56,800 --> 00:13:02,420
modeling the other's model, including the other's model of us. That recursive mutual modeling is an

151
00:13:02,420 --> 00:13:07,760
arms race with no natural ceiling, and once a brain has machinery for modeling other minds,

152
00:13:07,940 --> 00:13:13,700
it can turn that machinery on itself. Self-awareness, on this view, is theory of mind

153
00:13:13,700 --> 00:13:21,040
pointed inward. The idea has a surprising ancestor. In a section added to the gay science in 1887,

154
00:13:21,680 --> 00:13:25,920
Friedrich Nietzsche argued that consciousness developed only under the pressure of the need

155
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:31,280
which is why, he said, it is the most superficial part of the mind.

156
00:13:31,560 --> 00:13:35,080
You do not need his contempt to see the outline of modern theories,

157
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:39,320
including the attention schema theory we'll examine in episode 8.

158
00:13:40,640 --> 00:13:45,500
Aguera e. Arcus carries the continuum all the way to consciousness and free will,

159
00:13:45,720 --> 00:13:51,040
through a relational, social account, though his confidence is greatest about intelligence.

160
00:13:51,300 --> 00:13:54,300
We'll weigh his view of consciousness in episode 8,

161
00:13:54,300 --> 00:13:57,280
and the fourth test below targets that last step.

162
00:13:58,280 --> 00:14:01,920
Now the test, because a theory this sweeping deserves pressure.

163
00:14:02,260 --> 00:14:04,840
The first objection is pancomputationalism.

164
00:14:05,020 --> 00:14:10,040
In 1988, Hilary Putnam argued that, under a suitably contrived mapping,

165
00:14:10,460 --> 00:14:14,340
any ordinary physical system implements any finite state automaton you like.

166
00:14:14,760 --> 00:14:16,480
John Searle put it more memorably.

167
00:14:16,720 --> 00:14:21,580
The wall behind him, he said, could be described as running the word star word processor.

168
00:14:21,580 --> 00:14:29,120
If computation is that cheap, then life is computation and minds are computation become trivially true and say nothing.

169
00:14:29,460 --> 00:14:31,460
That always struck me as a trick.

170
00:14:31,780 --> 00:14:36,480
The mapping is invented after the fact, state by state, and it predicts nothing.

171
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,580
That's essentially David Chalmers' reply.

172
00:14:39,860 --> 00:14:46,200
In 1996, he published a paper asking whether a rock implements every finite state automaton.

173
00:14:46,960 --> 00:14:51,760
Real implementation, he argued, requires the right counterfactual structure.

174
00:14:51,980 --> 00:14:55,980
The physical system must be disposed to follow the computation's transitions,

175
00:14:56,340 --> 00:15:01,380
including in situations that never actually occur, and it must respond to inputs.

176
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:04,720
A wall has no such dispositions for word star.

177
00:15:05,020 --> 00:15:10,140
On that stricter notion, saying a cell computes is a substantive and testable claim,

178
00:15:10,300 --> 00:15:12,880
and a cell copying its DNA passes it.

179
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:14,400
The wall does not.

180
00:15:16,200 --> 00:15:21,760
about purpose. The cybernetic slogan that the purpose of a system is what it does makes every

181
00:15:21,760 --> 00:15:27,420
effect a purpose, so the purpose of the heart would include making thumping noises. Philosophers

182
00:15:27,420 --> 00:15:32,720
of biology like Ruth Milliken distinguish an effect from a function, an effect that explains

183
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:39,060
why the trait was selected. The evolutionary story can supply that distinction. The slogan alone

184
00:15:39,060 --> 00:15:44,680
cannot. The third objection targets the claim that language models mark a new major transition

185
00:15:46,200 --> 00:15:53,700
In 2025, Henry Farrell, Alison Gopnik, Cosma Shalizi, and James Evans argued in science that

186
00:15:53,700 --> 00:15:59,420
large models are better understood as cultural and social technologies, like writing, print,

187
00:15:59,740 --> 00:16:05,720
markets, and bureaucracies. On their view, these models are powerful new ways for humans to store,

188
00:16:06,080 --> 00:16:11,320
access, and recombine what other humans have produced, rather than new agents in their own

189
00:16:11,320 --> 00:16:17,060
right. That reading can grant that these systems are superb predictors, while denying that a new

190
00:16:17,060 --> 00:16:22,040
kind of mind has appeared. And the fourth objection is the one that matters most for this series,

191
00:16:22,460 --> 00:16:27,760
the slide from intelligence to consciousness. The first steps of the argument, that life

192
00:16:27,760 --> 00:16:33,940
involves computation, that intelligence is largely prediction, and that minds were shaped by social

193
00:16:33,940 --> 00:16:40,360
modeling, all have serious defenders, though each is contested. The final step, that a system with

194
00:16:41,320 --> 00:16:46,940
and social modeling thereby has experience is exactly what biological naturalists and

195
00:16:46,940 --> 00:16:54,220
integrated information theory deny, as we'll see in Episodes 9 and 13. Aguera E. Arcus's relational

196
00:16:54,220 --> 00:17:00,600
view is a coherent answer, but it's one that a dualist, a pansychist, or anil seth would reject

197
00:17:00,600 --> 00:17:08,260
at the root. So how much of the continuum survives the tests? Most of the first three steps. The

198
00:17:08,260 --> 00:17:14,320
computational view of life is more than a metaphor. Von Neumann's architecture closely parallels how

199
00:17:14,320 --> 00:17:20,280
cells reproduce, and the BFF soup shows that replication can emerge from computation plus

200
00:17:20,280 --> 00:17:26,600
interaction without design. Regulation requires modeling, and physics penalizes memories that do

201
00:17:26,600 --> 00:17:32,300
not predict, so prediction sits at the heart of anything that keeps itself going. And the hardest

202
00:17:32,300 --> 00:17:38,100
prediction problem many animals face is other animals, which may be why intelligence, and

203
00:17:38,260 --> 00:17:40,060
Perhaps the self has social roots.

204
00:17:40,340 --> 00:17:43,980
What the view does not settle and cannot settle on its own terms

205
00:17:43,980 --> 00:17:46,440
is whether any of this computation is felt.

206
00:17:46,720 --> 00:17:48,340
That's for the rest of the series.

207
00:17:48,740 --> 00:17:52,240
First, we follow prediction into the organ that does it best.

208
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:54,940
Next episode, the predictive brain.

